00:01:22.520 --> 00:01:26.520
So three minutes on your
entrepreneurial journey context

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:29.220
the, the 180 second, 242nd version

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:31.363
of kids selling baseball cards

00:01:31.363 --> 00:01:33.980
to where you are now at Walmart.

00:01:33.980 --> 00:01:36.710
- Yeah, when I, when I
was four years old I-

00:01:36.710 --> 00:01:38.440
people asked what I
wanted to be, the teacher.

00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:39.957
And I wrote this thing up that said,

00:01:39.957 --> 00:01:42.300
"I want to be a farmer
because they grow stuff

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:44.840
from nothing" in crayon
and my mom kept it.

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:47.840
And that basically sums
up, you know, everything.

00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:49.810
You know, I love creating
stuff from nothing.

00:01:49.810 --> 00:01:51.790
I did every entrepreneurial
business you could think

00:01:51.790 --> 00:01:52.810
about as a kid,

00:01:52.810 --> 00:01:56.610
mowing lawns, weeds,
newspapers, recycle, wash cars.

00:01:56.610 --> 00:01:57.443
- By the way, that's- that's

00:01:57.443 --> 00:01:59.650
that's, that's pulling
weeds in New Jersey,

00:01:59.650 --> 00:02:01.150
not selling weed as a kid, right?

00:02:01.150 --> 00:02:02.550
I just want to make sure
everybody heard you.

00:02:02.550 --> 00:02:03.760
- (laughing) Exactly.

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:06.109
- Though you're from Jersey,
so anything's possible.

00:02:06.109 --> 00:02:08.350
- [Marc] (laughing) That's true.

00:02:08.350 --> 00:02:09.770
And then, you know, went to college

00:02:09.770 --> 00:02:11.700
and there wasn't really the startup scene

00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:13.840
so when I graduated
college, I went to work

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:17.700
for a bank, you know, it's,
I wish I could have worked

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:19.700
for a startup, but it
wasn't really a thing.

00:02:19.700 --> 00:02:22.140
And then worked in banking for about six,

00:02:22.140 --> 00:02:24.390
seven years, was doing really well,

00:02:24.390 --> 00:02:25.920
knew I didn't want to
be in banking forever.

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:27.660
The startup scene was starting to heat up.

00:02:27.660 --> 00:02:30.951
This is late nineties and
decided one day to just quit

00:02:30.951 --> 00:02:34.810
and start a company and took some risk.

00:02:34.810 --> 00:02:35.740
And the rest is history.

00:02:35.740 --> 00:02:39.160
I've done four startups, sold-
built and sold four startups.

00:02:39.160 --> 00:02:40.260
And the last two-

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:42.873
One was to Amazon was Quidsi.

00:02:42.873 --> 00:02:45.410
It's the holding company
for diapers.com, wag.com.

00:02:45.410 --> 00:02:48.250
Sold to Amazon back in
2011, started jet.com.

00:02:48.250 --> 00:02:51.210
Sold that to Walmart uh four years ago.

00:02:51.210 --> 00:02:54.630
- Marc, can you give us
the five seconds version

00:02:54.630 --> 00:02:57.210
of how you got your
inventory for diapers.com

00:02:57.210 --> 00:02:59.610
which is a brand a lot of people know.

00:02:59.610 --> 00:03:00.443
- (laughing) Yeah.

00:03:00.443 --> 00:03:02.010
We started diapers.com.

00:03:02.010 --> 00:03:04.040
Yeah, Proctor and Gamble to Huggies,

00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:05.290
you know, Kimberly Clark.

00:03:06.730 --> 00:03:08.770
Pampers and Huggies are
the two main diaper brands,

00:03:08.770 --> 00:03:11.000
could not get them direct
from the manufacturer

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:11.833
for two years.

00:03:11.833 --> 00:03:13.070
They would not sell us.

00:03:13.070 --> 00:03:14.550
So the only way to get diapers was to go

00:03:14.550 --> 00:03:16.590
to the wholesale clubs and buy them.

00:03:16.590 --> 00:03:18.170
And so we'd go to the wholesale club,

00:03:18.170 --> 00:03:20.070
buy them, label them, ship them out.

00:03:20.070 --> 00:03:21.740
The problem was we lost money

00:03:21.740 --> 00:03:24.220
on every box of diapers we sold

00:03:24.220 --> 00:03:26.200
knowing full well that one day

00:03:26.200 --> 00:03:29.470
if we got big enough, the
manufacturer would sell us direct.

00:03:29.470 --> 00:03:32.900
And although, I mean, they
eventually did, but only

00:03:32.900 --> 00:03:36.390
because you know, the wholesale
club, I think it was BJ's.

00:03:36.390 --> 00:03:39.360
We would clear them out of
every single box of diapers.

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:41.607
And they got so frustrated that
they called the manufacturer

00:03:41.607 --> 00:03:43.920
and said, could you please
sell these guys direct,

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:45.200
because they're killing me.

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:46.640
And that's the only reason.

00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:49.184
And the manufacturer said at the time,

00:03:49.184 --> 00:03:52.420
We still don't believe
in your business model.

00:03:52.420 --> 00:03:54.560
We don't believe in
selling diapers online,

00:03:54.560 --> 00:03:56.970
but we'll sell you because
BJ's is an important client

00:03:56.970 --> 00:03:57.803
of ours.

00:03:57.803 --> 00:03:59.010
And that was the only reason.

00:03:59.010 --> 00:04:03.420
And, you know, fast forward,
you know, 10 years later

00:04:03.420 --> 00:04:06.720
Proctor and Gamble is basically
saying that they think that,

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:08.980
you know, why would anybody buy
diapers anywhere but online?

00:04:08.980 --> 00:04:10.310
Why would they go in a store?

00:04:10.310 --> 00:04:13.990
And so it's a very quick
turnaround, you know?

00:04:13.990 --> 00:04:17.050
- You sell a company to
Amazon, and then you come back

00:04:17.050 --> 00:04:19.670
almost a decade later and
you sell one to Walmart.

00:04:19.670 --> 00:04:24.330
And, and my question is, how do you-

00:04:24.330 --> 00:04:26.950
How do you bring your entrepreneurial-ism

00:04:26.950 --> 00:04:28.590
to the table in those environments?

00:04:28.590 --> 00:04:30.290
Right? One thing I've been impressed with-

00:04:30.290 --> 00:04:33.820
So, you know, being a fan
of your execution from afar,

00:04:33.820 --> 00:04:35.240
we didn't know each
other during this time,

00:04:35.240 --> 00:04:36.690
but we were doing our things in Jersey,

00:04:36.690 --> 00:04:38.560
and I'd always kind of root for you

00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:41.900
because there's so few people
who are actually operators.

00:04:41.900 --> 00:04:44.690
You know, by the way, back to
real entrepreneurs versus not,

00:04:44.690 --> 00:04:46.820
a lot of real entrepreneurs give up

00:04:46.820 --> 00:04:48.200
when they can't get the inventory

00:04:48.200 --> 00:04:49.300
'cause the business plan said,

00:04:49.300 --> 00:04:51.260
you have to buy the
product that, you know,

00:04:51.260 --> 00:04:53.250
that's what I admired.

00:04:53.250 --> 00:04:56.630
I, so when I saw the
jet.com, this me personally,

00:04:56.630 --> 00:04:58.760
official, you know, point
of view, I was like,

00:04:58.760 --> 00:05:00.750
okay, what a brilliant-

00:05:00.750 --> 00:05:02.360
He played the model again.

00:05:02.360 --> 00:05:04.210
He went to the other place.

00:05:04.210 --> 00:05:06.490
Is he really gonna be
able to impact that place?

00:05:06.490 --> 00:05:11.040
'Cause unlike the first place,
the DNA's matched way more.

00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:13.050
I'm real curious as a-

00:05:13.050 --> 00:05:13.883
I'm a fan.

00:05:13.883 --> 00:05:16.330
The way people watch basketball
is the way I watch business.

00:05:16.330 --> 00:05:18.490
We have Jeff, Jeff Smith coming up soon,

00:05:18.490 --> 00:05:20.570
who's also back to that whole ecosystem,

00:05:20.570 --> 00:05:22.520
an incredible executive at J and J.

00:05:22.520 --> 00:05:24.930
And as he went into his
entrepreneurial world,

00:05:24.930 --> 00:05:27.450
A, I care about him as a
human and was rooting for him

00:05:27.450 --> 00:05:32.000
but also curious to see that
kind of athlete who dominated

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:33.520
in that one game.

00:05:33.520 --> 00:05:34.510
How's he gonna be?

00:05:34.510 --> 00:05:35.360
Like, what's he gonna learn?

00:05:35.360 --> 00:05:37.140
What's he gonna be good
at and not good at?

00:05:37.140 --> 00:05:38.333
I love that shit.

00:05:39.210 --> 00:05:40.070
I was real-

00:05:40.070 --> 00:05:44.480
I really was like, ooh, can
Walmart ingest that organ?

00:05:44.480 --> 00:05:47.550
Can that body ingest that organ?

00:05:47.550 --> 00:05:48.680
And what's going to happen?

00:05:48.680 --> 00:05:51.370
And will jet.com eventually
become walmart.com?

00:05:51.370 --> 00:05:53.320
Some of these stories have played out.

00:05:53.320 --> 00:05:55.270
We've never talked about this
even though in the last couple

00:05:55.270 --> 00:05:57.040
of years we've gotten to know each other.

00:05:57.040 --> 00:05:59.840
Talk me through being
an entrepreneur operator

00:05:59.840 --> 00:06:01.700
in such a conglomerate.

00:06:01.700 --> 00:06:04.240
And what- Did it play out
better than you thought?

00:06:04.240 --> 00:06:06.950
Like, I'm sure you were-

00:06:06.950 --> 00:06:08.770
the body as well.

00:06:08.770 --> 00:06:10.510
You know, just using your common sense.

00:06:10.510 --> 00:06:11.810
I already kind of got your quick answer.

00:06:11.810 --> 00:06:13.460
I'm fascinated by this part,

00:06:13.460 --> 00:06:15.650
because this usually doesn't work out.

00:06:15.650 --> 00:06:18.080
It seemingly, I don't have
the details, is working out.

00:06:18.080 --> 00:06:19.410
Talking me through it.

00:06:19.410 --> 00:06:21.410
- Yeah, no, it's, it's
a, it's a great lesson

00:06:21.410 --> 00:06:23.880
for big companies making
acquisitions of startups.

00:06:23.880 --> 00:06:27.823
I think, you know, when, when
we sold the company to Amazon

00:06:27.823 --> 00:06:31.210
they were like, hey, you
continue to keep doing your thing

00:06:31.210 --> 00:06:32.430
because that's what you want to do, right?

00:06:32.430 --> 00:06:33.960
As an entrepreneur, you just
want to keep doing your thing.

00:06:33.960 --> 00:06:35.200
We'll leave you alone.

00:06:35.200 --> 00:06:36.570
And I think a lot of big companies think

00:06:36.570 --> 00:06:38.760
that entrepreneurs want to
just keep doing their thing

00:06:38.760 --> 00:06:40.040
and be left alone.

00:06:40.040 --> 00:06:41.330
That's not necessarily the case.

00:06:41.330 --> 00:06:42.840
When you have a platform as big as Amazon

00:06:42.840 --> 00:06:44.270
or as big as Walmart,

00:06:44.270 --> 00:06:46.820
you want to be able to
impact the mothership

00:06:46.820 --> 00:06:50.140
and really be able to
help drive massive change,

00:06:50.140 --> 00:06:51.940
much bigger than you can do on your own.

00:06:51.940 --> 00:06:54.970
So the case of Amazon, they left us alone.

00:06:54.970 --> 00:06:56.550
They said, keep doing your thing.

00:06:56.550 --> 00:06:58.450
You guys will be happy, just
doing your thing, right?

00:06:58.450 --> 00:07:01.450
And we're like, no, but nothing changed.

00:07:01.450 --> 00:07:03.240
With Walmart I had learned that lesson

00:07:03.240 --> 00:07:06.090
and talking to Doug McMillon,
the CEO at the time,

00:07:06.090 --> 00:07:10.410
who still is, really started to, to build

00:07:10.410 --> 00:07:12.400
you know, the, the, the core
values of the relationship

00:07:12.400 --> 00:07:13.350
from the ground up.

00:07:13.350 --> 00:07:15.410
You know, starting with trust.

00:07:15.410 --> 00:07:18.410
And we built a lot of trust in
a very short period of time.

00:07:18.410 --> 00:07:21.500
And I told Doug, if you
really want, you know,

00:07:21.500 --> 00:07:25.040
Jet, and me, and the
team to, to help Walmart

00:07:25.040 --> 00:07:27.770
and help, you know, drive a change,

00:07:27.770 --> 00:07:28.810
then we would be up for that.

00:07:28.810 --> 00:07:31.020
If you just want to buy
us and leave us alone

00:07:31.020 --> 00:07:33.360
and have us do our thing,
we're not interested.

00:07:33.360 --> 00:07:34.430
- We've done that already.

00:07:34.430 --> 00:07:37.660
And I don't want to play that dance again.

00:07:37.660 --> 00:07:39.640
- Exactly. And so when Doug
and the board came back

00:07:39.640 --> 00:07:42.280
and said, hey, we want to
give you and your team sort

00:07:42.280 --> 00:07:45.440
of the keys to e-commerce here at Walmart.

00:07:45.440 --> 00:07:48.670
We, we, we think that you and the team

00:07:48.670 --> 00:07:51.040
could really help make a big impact here.

00:07:51.040 --> 00:07:52.490
And I think that was the key.

00:07:52.490 --> 00:07:55.337
It was as simple as that, it
was basically empowering me

00:07:55.337 --> 00:07:58.490
and the team to go and make change.

00:07:58.490 --> 00:08:00.060
- A little bit more of
a hard ball question,

00:08:00.060 --> 00:08:02.130
How do you feel it's
going on, quote, unquote

00:08:02.130 --> 00:08:05.839
closing the gap between
amazon.com and walmart.com?

00:08:05.839 --> 00:08:08.280
- I mean, I think it's going really well.

00:08:08.280 --> 00:08:09.840
There's still a big gap there,

00:08:09.840 --> 00:08:11.610
but if you look at where
we were four years ago

00:08:11.610 --> 00:08:12.730
when I joined versus

00:08:12.730 --> 00:08:15.340
you know, where, where we are today,

00:08:15.340 --> 00:08:18.170
I'm- I'm quite proud of the
progress the team has made.

00:08:18.170 --> 00:08:20.350
- Where, where, where
have you advanced more

00:08:20.350 --> 00:08:21.660
than you anticipated?

00:08:21.660 --> 00:08:22.493
You're a smart dude.

00:08:22.493 --> 00:08:24.090
I've really come to admire you.

00:08:24.090 --> 00:08:27.060
Where have you advanced
more than you thought?

00:08:27.060 --> 00:08:29.800
And where have you fallen
short more than you thought,

00:08:29.800 --> 00:08:31.510
if I went into a time machine

00:08:31.510 --> 00:08:34.400
and had a glass of wine
with 48 months ago, Mark?

00:08:34.400 --> 00:08:36.280
- Yeah, I mean, as an entrepreneur,

00:08:36.280 --> 00:08:37.750
when I say, fall short, you know,

00:08:37.750 --> 00:08:42.750
I had really big audacious
goals set for ourselves

00:08:43.207 --> 00:08:45.150
and the team when we came in.

00:08:45.150 --> 00:08:46.490
You know, shoot for the stars

00:08:46.490 --> 00:08:47.600
and land on the moon sort of thing.

00:08:47.600 --> 00:08:52.470
So yeah, I mean, I think it
was harder than I expected,

00:08:52.470 --> 00:08:53.930
even though I expected it to be hard.

00:08:53.930 --> 00:08:54.780
It's hard.

00:08:54.780 --> 00:08:57.730
It's hard to move an aircraft
carrier and change direction.

00:08:58.760 --> 00:09:02.000
With that being said,
you know, I'm surprised

00:09:02.000 --> 00:09:03.950
at how much we've been
able to innovate inside

00:09:03.950 --> 00:09:05.160
of a big company.

00:09:05.160 --> 00:09:06.590
You know, we, we started this thing

00:09:06.590 --> 00:09:09.910
called Store No. 8, which
is an umbrella for startups

00:09:09.910 --> 00:09:11.630
that we built inside of Walmart.

00:09:11.630 --> 00:09:13.900
And we've been able to build
a bunch of these startups

00:09:13.900 --> 00:09:17.080
that are wholly owned by Walmart
and then infuse them back

00:09:17.080 --> 00:09:17.913
in the business.

00:09:17.913 --> 00:09:19.860
And I- It was a great lesson.

00:09:19.860 --> 00:09:21.830
I'd recommend it for any big company.

00:09:21.830 --> 00:09:24.550
You know, it's really hard
to build a startup inside

00:09:24.550 --> 00:09:26.340
of a business when, when
they're being managed

00:09:26.340 --> 00:09:28.334
by the operators of the business, because-

00:09:28.334 --> 00:09:30.310
- Impossi- It's two different agendas

00:09:30.310 --> 00:09:33.060
that operator cares
about short-term results.

00:09:33.060 --> 00:09:35.010
A startup needs to be like a baby.

00:09:35.010 --> 00:09:38.060
Those first three years you
have to coddle and be patient.

00:09:38.060 --> 00:09:39.250
- Yes, exactly.

00:09:39.250 --> 00:09:42.490
So if you can carve it out
and give it capital and

00:09:42.490 --> 00:09:44.390
and let it act like a startup.

00:09:44.390 --> 00:09:46.760
And then in a couple of
years, when it's got traction,

00:09:46.760 --> 00:09:48.440
the business then raises
their hand and says,

00:09:48.440 --> 00:09:50.960
yeah, I would love that
to be part of my business.

00:09:50.960 --> 00:09:52.610
And you're like, okay, here you go.

00:09:52.610 --> 00:09:54.010
You know, just take the winners.

00:09:54.010 --> 00:09:55.660
And, and it's worked out really well.

00:09:55.660 --> 00:09:56.600
We built some businesses.

00:09:56.600 --> 00:09:59.000
We infused them back in the business.

00:09:59.000 --> 00:10:02.140
I think the whole in-home delivery is, is

00:10:02.140 --> 00:10:04.630
is a great example, delivering directly

00:10:04.630 --> 00:10:06.740
into people's refrigerators.

00:10:06.740 --> 00:10:11.400
I think Walmart Plus launch,
I think, you know, that was a

00:10:11.400 --> 00:10:14.230
you know, a way for Walmart to
leverage their unique assets

00:10:14.230 --> 00:10:15.990
to do something that only Walmart can do.

00:10:15.990 --> 00:10:18.420
- [Gary] 100%.
- So...

00:10:20.000 --> 00:10:24.470
- What, what's your biggest
